{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Batch overlap-judge candidates per call: cut call volume, and grow the prefix past 1024\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nStory `8415-b6e8-c206-48f6` moved the batch-invariant query digest out of the per-call user\nmessage into the overlap-judge system prompt, so the shared bytes sit in the block the runner\nalready puts a cache breakpoint on. That relocation is done and measured. It does not pay off,\nbecause the resulting prefix does not reach the model's minimum cacheable prefix.\n\nMeasured on real `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` calls (the `standard` class `overlap-judge` runs\non post-`afeb`), one query ticket and six candidate digests, 12 calls:\n\n    stable system prefix   before  423 tokens        after  846 tokens      floor  1024\n    input tokens           before  16,040            after  16,976          (+936, +5.8%)\n    cache_read_tokens      before  0                 after  0\n    cache_write_tokens     before  0                 after  0\n    verdict parity         relations identical on 12/12 ordered pairs\n\nThe floor is measured, not assumed: through the real judge request path an 875-token system\nprefix produced write 0 / read 0 while a 1250-token one produced write 1243 then read 1243.\n`count_tokens` over the base prompt (423) plus each of seven real `rebar.llm.enrich` digests\n(306-453) gives 728-875 — every one short of 1024 by **149-296 tokens**. So the relocation costs\n~5.8% more input and returns nothing, and 8415 is held until this ticket makes the pair\nnet-positive.\n\n## Approach\n\nJudge N candidates in ONE call per ordering instead of one call per candidate per ordering.\nToday `judge()` issues 2N calls, each re-sending the whole system prefix. Batched, it issues\n`2 * ceil(N / batch)` calls: the query digest stays hoisted in the system block (unchanged from\n8415), and the user message carries a LABELLED LIST of candidate digests instead of one.\n\n**Two mechanisms, and they are NOT the same mechanism. State them separately, because one is\narithmetic and the other is a bet.**\n\n1. **Call-count reduction — arithmetic, and the mechanism the ticket actually rests on.** Every\n   avoided call removes one whole copy of the 846-token prefix from the bill. At N=6 with one\n   batch per ordering, 12 calls become 2: ten prefix copies (~8,460 tokens) disappear outright.\n   This requires no caching whatsoever and cannot fail to reduce total input tokens; only its\n   magnitude is uncertain, because the candidate digests themselves are re-sent either way.\n\n2. **Prefix growth past 1024 — a bet that must be measured, and batching ALONE does not deliver\n   it.** The cacheable prefix is the *system block*: base prompt + hoisted query digest. Putting\n   more candidates in the *user* message does not add a single token to it. Batching lifts the\n   prefix only because the batch protocol has to be described somewhere, and it belongs in the\n   system prompt: per-candidate independence, the labelled-list input shape, one echoed verdict\n   per candidate. That section is required by the change, not padding invented to reach a number\n   — but whether base 423 + section + digest 306-453 clears 1024 for every real digest is an\n   empirical question this ticket answers with `count_tokens`, and it may come out short for the\n   smallest digests. A prefix that still does not cache is a valid recorded outcome; mechanism 1\n   carries the ticket in that case.\n\nDeliberately NOT done: no padding is added to reach the floor (buying a cache with the tokens it\nwas meant to save is not a saving), `capabilities.py`'s `CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` is not touched\n(that is ticket `e3cd`), and the loop is not parallelized (fanning out cold calls that share a\nprefix turns one write plus reads into N premium writes — strictly worse; 8415 records this).\n\n**The cost is precision, and it is a real cost.** `reviewers/overlap_judge.md` is written for ONE\nORDERED PAIR — \"FIRST <relation> SECOND\", a closed five-value relation set, a required named\n`shared_artifact`, `related_distinct` as the default, \"false flags are costly\". Judging several\ncandidates in one call risks cross-contamination: a shared artifact cited for one candidate\nbleeding into the verdict for another, or an unrelated candidate dragged toward\n`related_distinct` by its neighbours. This is the real risk of the change and it is MEASURED, not\nasserted, by the parity run below.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/overlap/judge.py` — batch construction (a labelled candidate list, the batch-size\n  bound) and per-candidate response demultiplexing. `judge_one` and the un-batched request shape\n  stay as the single-pair path, unedited.\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/overlap_judge.md` — a BATCH section describing one FIRST against a\n  labelled list of SECOND candidates (and the mirrored ordering), per-candidate independence, and\n  one echoed verdict per candidate. The single-pair instructions above it are unchanged.\n- `src/rebar/llm/contracts.py` — a new `overlap_verdict_batch` contract: a list of per-candidate\n  entries, each carrying `candidate_id` plus today's four `overlap_verdict` fields. Registered\n  next to `overlap_verdict`, which keeps its registration and its single-pair use.\n- `tests/unit/test_overlap_judge.py` — batch payload shape, demultiplexing, and fail-safe tests.\n\nNo `src/rebar/schemas/` change: `overlap_verdict_batch` is an internal contract with no `--output`\nsurface, like `plan_review_novelty` and `code_review_coach`, so no schema file is added and the\nevery-schema-file-is-wired guard is unaffected. No `config.py` change: the batch bound is a module\nconstant in `judge.py`, since no operator-facing tuning is claimed for it.\n\n## Testing\n\nOffline tests drive the real `judge()` through the existing recording fake runner, asserting on\nthe ACTUAL `RunRequest` payload rather than a stand-in.\n\n- **Call count** — 6 candidates yield strictly fewer `RunRequest`s than 12, and each ordering's\n  request carries every candidate's digest block, each under its own label.\n- **Both orderings survive batching** — in ordering 1 the hoisted reference occupies the FIRST\n  slot and the labelled candidate list the SECOND; in ordering 2 the reverse.\n- **Demultiplexing** — a canned batched response maps back to exactly one verdict per candidate,\n  matched by label and not by position; a candidate absent from the response, and a response\n  entry naming an unknown label, both resolve to abstain for the affected candidate only, leaving\n  the others' verdicts intact.\n- **Fail-safe** — a runner that raises, and a runner returning a malformed batch (not a dict / no\n  verdict list / entries missing their label), each yield `[]` from `judge()` with nothing\n  propagating.\n- **Batch bound** — a corpus larger than the bound is split into multiple calls per ordering, and\n  every candidate still receives exactly one verdict.\n- **Mutation check, both directions, for every new test:** each new test must FAIL with the\n  batching change reverted and PASS restored. Recorded on the ticket.\n- **Real-API parity and measurement** — the same query ticket and candidate corpus judged on\n  8415's per-pair build and on this batched build, relations compared pair by pair.\n- Gates: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and\n  `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 6 --dist worksteal -q`, judged by exit code.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The batched build issues strictly fewer calls than candidates x 2 for a corpus of >= 6\n      candidates, shown by a recording-runner test counting `RunRequest`s.\n- [ ] Every candidate receives exactly one verdict, matched by its label rather than by position;\n      an absent or unlabelled entry becomes an abstain for that candidate ONLY; checked by the\n      demultiplexing tests.\n- [ ] The step stays advisory: a raising runner and a malformed batched response each yield `[]`\n      from `judge()` with no exception escaping; checked by the fail-safe tests.\n- [ ] Precision is shown, not assumed: a real-API parity run over one query ticket and at least\n      six candidates reports per-pair `relation` for the per-pair build and the batched build, and\n      any differing relation is called out with its digests; recorded as a ticket comment.\n      Identical relations on every pair is the pass condition; a divergence is reported, not\n      averaged away.\n- [ ] A BEFORE/AFTER measurement on real `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` calls over the SAME query\n      ticket and SAME corpus records, for both builds, total `input_tokens`, `cache_read_tokens`,\n      `cache_write_tokens`, the call count, and the `count_tokens` size of the stable prefix beside\n      the measured 1024 floor.\n- [ ] The pair is judged against the PRE-8415 baseline, not just against 8415: the batched build's\n      total `input_tokens` must be below **16,040** on the same corpus, or the ticket reports the\n      pair as still net-negative in plain terms with the numbers. `cache_write_tokens` above one\n      prefix write per batch FAILS this criterion.\n- [ ] The prefix bet is reported either way: the batched `count_tokens` prefix is recorded against\n      1024 for the corpus's digest-size range, and a prefix that still does not clear the floor is\n      recorded as such rather than presented as a caching win.\n- [ ] Every new test is mutation-checked in both directions and the evidence recorded as a ticket\n      comment.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` all exit 0."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785711536562087001,"uuid":"148deaff-5277-48a8-915d-41c70a4cddc5"}